<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Adad-nirari III</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. c. 811–783 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century-bce">8th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Adad-nirari III was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a> from about 811 to 783 BCE, succeeding his father Shamshi-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadad">Adad</a> V while young, possibly under the influence of his mother Sammuramat. His campaigns to the west weakened Aram-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>, and a stele from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tell-al-rimah">Tell al-Rimah</a> records tribute from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jehoash-of-israel">Joash of Israel</a>, whose recovery of towns from the Arameans followed.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/adad-nirari-iii.jpg" alt="Adad-nirari III" width="320" height="564" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Osama Shukir Muhammed Amin FRCP(Glasg) · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Adad-nirari_III_transparent.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Adad-nirari III was a son and successor of Shamshi-Adad V, and his name means Adad, the storm god, is my help. He was apparently quite young at his accession, because for the first five years of his reign his mother Shammuramat was highly influential, which gave rise to the legend of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/semiramis">Semiramis</a>. It is widely rejected that she acted as regent, but she was surprisingly influential for the period. He was the father of the kings <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashur-nirari-v">Ashur-nirari V</a>, Shalmaneser IV and Ashur-dan III. Tiglath-Pileser III described himself in his inscriptions as a son of Adad-nirari, but it is uncertain whether this is true.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>His youth, and the struggles his father had faced early in his own reign, had seriously weakened Assyrian rulership over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> and made room for the ambitions of officers, governors and local rulers. According to his inscriptions he led several campaigns to regain the strength <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a> had enjoyed under his grandfather <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shalmaneser-iii">Shalmaneser III</a>. The eponym canon records that he campaigned in all directions until the last of his 28 years of reign, 783 BCE, and he built the temple of Nabu at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nineveh">Nineveh</a>.</p><p>Among his actions was a siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> in 796 BCE, in the time of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ben-hadad-iii">Ben-Hadad III</a>. It led to the eclipse of the Aramaean kingdom of Damascus and allowed the recovery of Israel under Jehoash, who paid the Assyrian king tribute at this time, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jeroboam-ii">Jeroboam II</a>. Despite his vigour, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a> entered a period of weakness lasting several decades after his death.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Adad-nirari III” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
